## 11. Core model and API policy for this pass

Do not implement or test live Muse API calls during this UI/UX redesign pass. Do not request or invent an API key. Use a `ModelProvider` abstraction with a clearly labelled local mock/demo provider and structured sample outputs for development. Mock results must be visibly marked as simulated and cannot be reported as verified real-world actions.

Keep integration points ready for later Meta model adapters, especially Muse Spark, Muse Wonder, Muse Image, Muse Voice Transcribe, SAM 3.1, Muse Glimmer, and Muse Spark Open. Later, Muse can refine the integrations and we will use valid credentials to test them. Make the provider settings page ready for future configuration, but do not fabricate live availability or responses.

## 12. Token and compute efficiency

- UI animation commands such as sit, rest, run and dance run locally from validated commands.
- Music playback, playlist operations, schedule timers, basic app discovery, rule storage, file operations, and simple calculations should use deterministic code.
- Make model calls only for natural-language interpretation, planning, complex decisions, app instruction help, or tasks where the model adds value.
- Use short task summaries and retrieve only relevant memories.
- Load plugin/tool schemas on demand rather than sending every tool definition for every request.
- Prefer app accessibility trees and selected text over screenshots. Only capture screenshots when necessary and authorised.
- Cache stable results and generated artwork; never regenerate the character on each launch.
- Avoid model calls for every animation frame, audio beat, clock tick, or scheduler poll.
- Respect quiet hours, focus mode, token/cost limits, and user-configured resource limits.
- Keep a clear distinction between simulated metrics and actual provider usage.

## 13. Security and safety requirements

- Keep credentials out of renderer code, logs, memory, plugin output and skill files.
- Separate untrusted web content, plugin output and generated code from trusted instructions.
- Validate all IPC, imported templates, plugin manifests, rules, skills and animation commands.
- Deny new plugin permissions by default.
- Run third-party plugin code in a restricted process or sandbox, never in the privileged main process.
- Do not read raw passwords, browser cookies, OTPs, PINs or secret keys.
- Use OS-native authentication for sensitive approval when available; voice or custom face matching alone must not authorise high-risk actions.
- Require explicit confirmation for destructive file operations, sending messages, purchases, external publication, sensitive account changes, and financial actions.
- Keep user rules editable without allowing them to bypass actual code-level protections.
- Provide pause, cancel, emergency stop, audit history, memory deletion and plugin removal.

