## 15. Acceptance criteria

The redesign is accepted only when evidence shows:

1. The companion is visibly an animated 2D anime girl on a transparent floating desktop window—not an AssistiveTouch button.
2. Morrow can idle naturally and respond to model/application events with the appropriate pose or movement without requiring a manual action button for each behaviour.
3. Direct prompts such as “sit”, “run over here”, “dance”, and “go rest” map to controlled local animations.
4. The user can name Morrow during first-run setup and change the name later.
5. Morrow is the only default personality. Users can download the template, import their edited personality, preview changes, export, reset, and remove user-added personalities.
6. Users can download a plugin template, import plugins, inspect permissions, test in a restricted environment, and remove plugins. Morrow can draft plugin/skill files, but installation requires user approval.
7. Users can manage their own rules and turn corrections into reviewed, versioned rules.
8. Blaize can discover authorised installed apps, explain how to use an app, and save a safe user-demonstrated workflow as a reusable skill.
9. The music player works with user-selected local files and playlists without model/API access, and Morrow can trigger dance mode from natural language.
10. Users can create, edit, pause, resume, and delete scheduled missions with time zone, conditions, limits, approval policy, and run history.
11. Basic animation, music, scheduling and storage paths do not depend on a language model.
12. No live Muse API call is made during this pass. Mock mode is labelled as simulated.
13. Existing working features are preserved or deliberately replaced with documented reasons.
14. Relevant unit/integration tests pass, and screenshots or a short screen recording demonstrate the redesigned desktop experience.

At the end, report exactly what was changed, which tests were run and their results, which features are real versus mocked, what remains incomplete, and how to run the application locally. Do not say a feature works if only its UI exists.